  "issue_title": "Request.url turns a // path into an authority when the scope has no host",
  "issue_body": "When a request scope has neither a `Host` header nor a `server` entry, `Request.url` places `scope[\"path\"]` directly after the scheme, so a path that starts with `//` is parsed as an authority. A response built from that URL then emits a scheme-relative `Location`.\n\n### Reproduction\n\n```python\nfrom starlette.requests import Request\nfrom starlette.responses import RedirectResponse\n\nscope = {\n    \"type\": \"http\",\n    \"scheme\": \"http\",\n    \"path\": \"//evil.example/x\",\n    \"query_string\": b\"a=1\",\n    \"headers\": [],\n    \"server\": None,\n}\n\nurl = Request(scope).url\nprint(str(url), url.netloc)\nprint(RedirectResponse(url).headers[\"location\"])\n```\n\nOn current `main` (1.7.0) this prints:\n\n```text\n//evil.example/x?a=1 evil.example\n//evil.example/x?a=1\n```\n\nThe `Location` value is scheme-relative, so a browser resolves it to `http://evil.example/x?a=1`.\n\nAs soon as any origin information is present the result is same-origin, which is the expected behaviour:\n\n```python\nscope[\"headers\"] = [(b\"host\", b\"victim.example\")]   # or scope[\"server\"] = (\"victim.example\", 80)\n# -> http://victim.example//evil.example/x?a=1\n```\n\n### Why this looks wrong\n\n#3326 says the path should not be placed directly against the authority and that the resulting URL should parse back into the components it was built from. That holds when `Host` or `server` is available, but the branch that builds the URL without origin metadata still uses the raw path as the whole URL, so the path ends up in `netloc`.\n\n`server` is optional in the ASGI HTTP scope, so a scope without it and without a `Host` header is legal. An application that redirects to `request.url` then sends the visitor to the host taken from the path.\n\n### Suggested fix\n\nIn the branch that builds the URL without origin metadata, keep a `//`-prefixed path out of the authority position, for example by percent-encoding its first character or by producing a URL without a netloc, so `urlsplit` cannot turn the first path segment into a host.\n",
